# Node modules
node_modules/
backend/node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/

# Environment variables
#
# The bare filenames alone were not enough: only `.env`, `backend/.env` and
# `frontend/.env` were ignored, so any variant — .env.production, .env.local,
# .env.prod.bak — was committable, which is exactly the file most likely to hold
# a production credential. The wildcards cover every variant; the negations keep
# the placeholder-only examples tracked.
#
# Each directory needs its OWN negation. `!.env.example` alone does not rescue
# `backend/.env.example`: gitignore applies the LAST matching pattern, and the
# anchored `backend/.env.*` below it wins — so the template would be silently
# untrackable, which is how a setup file goes missing without anyone noticing.
#
# NOTE: ignoring a path does nothing once the file is already tracked. These
# four were committed with `git add -f` in 426bcb6 and stayed tracked despite
# matching every rule here. If a secret lands in the index again, `git rm
# --cached <file>` is what actually stops it — not another line in this file.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
backend/.env
backend/.env.*
!backend/.env.example
frontend/.env
frontend/.env.*
!frontend/.env.example

# Build outputs
dist/
build/
.vite/

